# Which mill reads which file. `deck/deck/code/role.ts` reads this THROUGH
# THE ROLE MILL at `deck/mill/code/deck/role/`, and `deck/call/code/role-of.ts`
# hands the answer to the compiler, so a file's role overrides what its
# content says.
#
# `take` names a glob, `miss` excludes one, and `@` is the package root. A
# glob carrying braces escapes them (`\{code,view\}`), because a bare `{x}`
# is an interpolation to the parser. The escape belongs to the tree syntax and
# is resolved before the pattern reaches the matcher.
#
# THE ROLES THIS PACKAGE USES:
#
#   code    ordinary Term. The default when no rule matches
#   book    documentation
#   view    the sandboxed document dialect (note/term/view/)
#   host    a data file, compiled to its value rather than to a program
#   call    a file of CLI definitions: every `hook` in it is a COMMAND
#   site    a file of routes: every `hook` in it is a URL ROUTE
#
# `call` and `site` exist because `hook` spells both, and telling them apart
# from CONTENT is a guess: the rule used to look for a `view` child, so an API
# route (`hook /users` with `task get` and `task post`, no view) was built as a
# CLI command and its methods were collected into the wrong shape. A file that
# declares which it is cannot be guessed wrong. Without a rule the compiler
# still falls back to content, which is what every file here relies on today.

load @term/code
  find code
  find book

role book
  take @/book/**/*.tree
    miss @/book/**/\{code,view\}/**/*.tree

role code
  take @/code/**/*.tree
  take @/book/**/\{code,view\}/**/*.tree
